        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a vacuum with a palm sander?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Strongly recommended. Beyond the health benefit of capturing fine dust, extracting from under the disc stops loose grit being dragged across the surface, which is a significant cause of scratches at fine grits. Use a fine dust or HEPA filter in the vacuum.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What orbit stroke should I look for?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Around 2 to 3mm for furniture finishing, which sands finely at the cost of speed. Around 5mm for stock removal, deck sanding and paint stripping, which is faster but leaves a coarser pattern that needs finer grits afterwards. Most general purpose machines sit near 3mm.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Random orbit or sheet sander?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Random orbit sanders spin and oscillate simultaneously, leaving no directional scratch pattern, which is what allows an even finish. Quarter sheet sanders vibrate in a fixed small orbit, are gentler and cheaper, and their square pads reach into corners a round pad cannot.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do I get swirl marks?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually pressing down, which stalls the pad rotation so the machine orbits without spinning and leaves a directional pattern. Moving too fast and skipping grits are the other two causes. Let the weight of the machine do the work and move about an inch per second.",
