    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Palm sander is a loose term covering two quite different machines. Random orbit sanders spin and oscillate at once, which leaves no directional scratch pattern and makes them the standard finishing tool. Sheet sanders vibrate in a small orbit, are cheaper and gentler, and reach into corners a round pad cannot. Most workshops want the first and benefit from owning the second.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Random Orbit vs Sheet Sanders -->
        <div id="orbit-vs-sheet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Random Orbit vs Sheet Sanders
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two machines sold as palm sanders work differently, and choosing the wrong one leaves marks that only appear once the finish goes on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Random Orbit Spins and Oscillates</h3>

          <p>
            The pad rotates on a spindle that is itself offset, so every point on the abrasive follows a path that never repeats. There is no consistent direction to the scratches, which is why the surface takes a finish evenly with no visible pattern.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sheet Sanders Vibrate in a Fixed Orbit</h3>

          <p>
            A quarter sheet sander moves the pad in a small circle without rotating it, so the scratch pattern is consistent and fine. It is gentle and controllable, and under a clear finish that pattern can be faintly visible on some woods.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Round Pads Cannot Reach Corners</h3>

          <p>
            This is the practical division. A random orbit sander leaves an unfinished triangle in every inside corner and along every edge that meets another surface. A square sheet sander reaches into both, which is why the two tools complement each other.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Random Orbit Removes Material Faster</h3>

          <p>
            The rotation adds cutting action that a pure orbit lacks, so a random orbit machine takes stock down considerably more quickly. For flattening and for moving through grits on a panel, it is the far more efficient tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Own Both if You Finish Furniture</h3>

          <p>
            A five inch random orbit sander for panels and flats, and a quarter sheet sander for corners, edges and between finish coats. The sheet sander costs very little and eliminates a category of hand sanding entirely.
          </p>

