        <!-- SECTION 2: Orbit Stroke, Speed and What They Do -->
        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Orbit Stroke, Speed and What They Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The specifications printed on a palm sander box are largely uninformative except for two, and those two determine what the machine is good at.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Orbit stroke is the most important number.</strong> It is how far the pad moves off centre, typically between 2mm and 5mm. A small stroke sands finely and slowly; a large stroke removes material quickly and leaves a coarser pattern that needs finer grits to remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Choose stroke for your work, not the highest number.</strong> Furniture finishing wants a fine stroke around 2 to 3mm. Deck sanding, paint removal and shaping want 5mm. Most general purpose machines sit around 3mm as a compromise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Variable speed is genuinely useful.</strong> Lower speeds reduce heat, which prevents clogging on finishes and burning on resinous wood, and give more control on veneer and thin material. It is worth paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pad size affects reach as well as coverage.</strong> Five inch is standard and covers ground efficiently. Six inch machines exist for large surfaces and are unwieldy on small work. Six inch discs also cost more and are less widely stocked.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pad brake quality is worth checking.</strong> Without a brake, the pad free-spins when lifted and can dig a mark when it touches back down. Better machines slow it deliberately, which is the difference between a clean touchdown and a swirl to sand out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Amperage tells you very little.</strong> Sanding requires modest power, and every machine here has enough. Vibration, balance, stroke and dust collection all matter far more than the motor rating.
          </p>
        </div>

