        <!-- SECTION 4: Sanding Without Leaving Swirl Marks -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sanding Without Leaving Swirl Marks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swirl marks are the characteristic failure of random orbit sanding, and they are invisible on bare wood and glaringly obvious once stain or finish goes on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Move slowly, about an inch per second.</strong> The instinct is to sweep the sander around quickly, which leaves areas that have seen only a few random passes and therefore retain a pattern. A slow steady pass gives the random action time to do its job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not press down.</strong> Pressure stalls the pad rotation, at which point the machine is orbiting without spinning and leaving exactly the directional pattern the design exists to avoid. The weight of the machine is the correct pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the pad flat.</strong> Tilting concentrates the abrasive on one edge, which cuts a curved groove. This is the most common cause of visible marks on edges and near the perimeter of a panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never skip grits.</strong> Each grit removes the scratches of the previous one. Jumping from 80 to 180 leaves 80 grit scratches that the finer paper polishes around rather than removes, and they appear under finish. Step through in sensible increments.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start and stop with the pad on the work.</strong> Switching on with the sander in the air lets the pad reach full speed, and setting it down spinning marks the surface. Place it flat, then start; stop, then lift.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wipe with mineral spirits to check.</strong> A rag dampened with mineral spirits darkens the surface temporarily and reveals every scratch and swirl exactly as a finish would. It evaporates completely, so it costs nothing to check before committing to a finish.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Mineral Spirits Test</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Before you finish anything, wipe the surface with mineral spirits. It simulates exactly what a finish will look like, showing every swirl, scratch and glue spot you cannot see on dry bare wood. It evaporates without affecting the finish afterwards. Almost every disappointing finish would have been caught by this ten second check.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Abrasives and Getting Value From Them -->
        <div id="discs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Abrasives and Getting Value From Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sanding discs are a consumable that people routinely use long past their useful life, which costs more in time than the discs would have cost to replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A worn disc polishes rather than cuts.</strong> Once the abrasive is dull the machine burnishes the surface, which looks smooth and then takes stain unevenly because the pores are closed. Change discs far more often than feels necessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Step through grits in sensible increments.</strong> A typical progression runs 80, 120, 180 and finishes at 220 for most furniture. Going beyond 220 before staining can close the grain enough to affect colour uptake.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match hole patterns for dust extraction.</strong> Discs must have holes aligned with the pad for extraction to work. Mismatched discs block the extraction path entirely, which both fills the workshop and lets dust stay under the disc, causing swirls.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ceramic and zirconia last longer on hardwood.</strong> They cost more per disc and less per hour because they fracture to expose fresh cutting edges rather than simply dulling. On oak and maple the difference is substantial.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean discs with a rubber stick.</strong> A crepe rubber cleaning block held against a running disc pulls out the loaded resin and finish. Many discs that seem finished are simply clogged, and cleaning restores most of the cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Replace the hook and loop pad when discs stop gripping.</strong> The pad surface wears out before the machine does, and a disc that slips loses much of its effect while feeling roughly normal. Pads are inexpensive and easy to change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Matching the Sander to the Job -->
        <div id="projects" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching the Sander to the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same machine behaves very differently across materials, and a few job-specific rules save a great deal of rework.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bare hardwood wants a full grit progression.</strong> Start at 80 or 100 depending on how rough the stock is, and step to 220. Oak and ash show every skipped grit under a clear finish because the open grain exaggerates any scratch running across it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Veneer and plywood need care and a low speed.</strong> The face veneer on modern plywood can be less than a millimetre thick, and a random orbit sander removes that in seconds at an edge. Stay at 150 grit and above, keep the pad flat, and never linger near a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Painted surfaces load discs fast.</strong> Paint softens with heat and clogs abrasive almost immediately. Running at a lower speed keeps the temperature down, and a sheet sander with cheap cut sheet paper is far more economical than getting through discs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Between finish coats, sand lightly at 320 or finer.</strong> The aim is knocking down dust nibs and raised grain, not removing the coat. A quarter sheet sander or a hand block is usually better here, because a random orbit machine can cut through a thin coat before you notice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Old finishes may contain lead.</strong> Anything painted before the late 1970s should be tested before sanding, because a power sander turns lead paint into fine airborne dust extremely efficiently. A positive test means chemical stripping or a professional, not a sander.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>End grain drinks finish and needs finer sanding.</strong> Sanding end grain a grit or two beyond the face closes it slightly and stops it absorbing stain far darker than the surrounding wood, which is the usual cause of blotchy ends on a tabletop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Dust Collection on a Palm Sander -->
        <div id="dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dust Collection on a Palm Sander
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sanding dust is the largest health exposure in most home workshops, and palm sanders produce a great deal of the fine fraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Onboard bags and canisters are partial solutions.</strong> They capture visible dust and let the fine fraction through. Useful when a vacuum cannot follow you, but not a substitute for one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A vacuum transforms both dust and finish quality.</strong> Beyond the health benefit, extracting dust from under the disc stops loose grit being dragged across the surface, which is a significant cause of scratches at fine grits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a fine filter in the vacuum.</strong> A standard shop vacuum filter passes fine wood dust and exhausts it into the room. A HEPA or fine dust filter is what makes the extraction genuinely protective rather than merely tidy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hose weight affects control.</strong> A heavy vacuum hose dragging on a palm sander pulls it off flat and causes edge marks. Supporting the hose from above, or using a lighter antistatic hose, removes the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hardwood dust is a recognised carcinogen.</strong> Oak and beech dust in particular are classified as carcinogenic in several jurisdictions, which makes a rated respirator rather than a paper mask the correct precaution for extended sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the air settle before finishing.</strong> Fine dust hangs for hours and lands on wet finish, producing a gritty surface. Finishing in the same room immediately after sanding is a common and entirely avoidable cause of a ruined topcoat.
          </p>
        </div>

