              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Random orbit or sheet sander?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Random orbit sanders spin and oscillate simultaneously, leaving no directional scratch pattern, which is what allows an even finish. Quarter sheet sanders vibrate in a fixed small orbit, are gentler and cheaper, and their square pads reach into corners a round pad cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do I get swirl marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually pressing down, which stalls the pad rotation so the machine orbits without spinning and leaves a directional pattern. Moving too fast and skipping grits are the other two causes. Let the weight of the machine do the work and move about an inch per second.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grit should I finish at?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 180 to 220 for most furniture taking a stain or clear finish. Going finer closes the grain and can make stain absorb unevenly, producing a blotchy result. Painted work can stop at 150, since primer fills the remaining scratches.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Strongly recommended. Beyond the health benefit of capturing fine dust, extracting from under the disc stops loose grit being dragged across the surface, which is a significant cause of scratches at fine grits. Use a fine dust or HEPA filter in the vacuum.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What orbit stroke is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 2 to 3mm for furniture finishing, which sands finely at the cost of speed. Around 5mm for stock removal, deck sanding and paint stripping, which is faster but leaves a coarser pattern that needs finer grits afterwards. Most general purpose machines sit near 3mm.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

