          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Palm Sanding Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every sanding complaint traces back to one of four habits, and all of them show up only once the finish goes on.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing Down on the Sander</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pressure stalls the pad's rotation, at which point the machine is orbiting without spinning and leaving exactly the directional scratches that random orbit action exists to prevent. It also generates heat that clogs the disc. The weight of the tool is the correct downforce, and adding more slows the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Grits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each grit exists to remove the scratches left by the previous one. Jumping from 80 to 180 means the fine paper polishes around the coarse scratches rather than removing them, and those scratches appear the moment stain or finish goes on. Step through in sensible increments.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Worn Discs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull disc burnishes rather than cuts, closing the wood pores so stain absorbs unevenly and producing a blotchy finish. It also takes far longer to achieve nothing. Discs are cheap relative to the time wasted, and changing them more often than feels necessary is correct.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking With Mineral Spirits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bare wood hides swirl marks, scratches and glue residue completely, and all three become obvious under finish. Wiping the surface with mineral spirits simulates exactly what the finish will show, evaporates without trace, and takes ten seconds. Almost every disappointing finish would have been caught by it.
              </p>
            </div>
